Nice!  Yep, that's exactly the kind of thing I had in mind.

 

And sure, any of the parameters in your model, including the other effects and random effect variances, could be quite different in "reality" (i.e., the larger population) from what you observed.  And so you could run some more alternatives checking some different sets of assumptions.  But I think given this current example, the thing to worry most about is the possibility that the effect size of 1.9 from your 80-person study could be an overestimate, so I think what you've done here is a very reasonable step. In other words, I think the particular assumption that the effect you care about (the interaction) is accurate is the most dangerous for the purposes of estimating N sizes for power, so playing with that one, exactly as you've done here, is a really useful step.

 

In terms of the covariate thing, I say just keep playing with some simulations, and that'll probably be more immediately helpful than the literature :-)

 

best,

-scott

 

 

