Hi Cai,

this is an annoying behavior of the aov function when the design is not perfectly balanced and there are missing cells. So it seems for at least one participant you do not have data from both factor levels of cLength. Then aov assumes that cLength is a within factor for those participants for whom you have both data points and a between factor for the ones with only one cell.
Of course, unbalanced data sets are no problem for LMEs but for ANOVAs you would have to remove the participants with missing cells (at least for within-subject designs) before running the ANOVA.

You could use the EZ package. It will give out a warning in these cases. It also lets you plot the number of data points for each design cell.

Best,
Tom

