References: <024001d0b8b9$15af3990$410dacb0$@gmail.com> <CAGLQsKcXpzs90-gUuPcMuEHxCa4VGHSRP=tfhABWYi+4Co5s=A@mail.gmail.com> <024d01d0b8bf$f15b9640$d412c2c0$@gmail.com>
Message-ID: <559BE4E1.5030304@glasgow.ac.uk>

Zhenguang,

With 1400+ observations on 59 units, you really should have enough data 
to estimate all seven random effects parameters.  However, your 
predictors are all on very different scales. You have:

Exp:      [1, 2]
Duration: [1000, 2600]
Length:   [150, 550]

The LMM converges if you re-scale your predictors.  I would suggest:

E12L$cExp = E12L$cExp - mean(E12L$cExp)
E12L$cDuration = (E12L$Duration - mean(E12L$Duration)) / 1600
E12L$cLength = (E12L$Length - mean(E12L$Length)) / 400

-Dale Barr

