On 12.06.2015 19:38, Scott Jackson wrote:
> In terms of the covariate thing, I say just keep playing with some 
> simulations, and that'll probably be more immediately helpful than the 
> literature :-)

I've taken your advice on board and have run some simulations.
For the moment, I've only considered 'normal' (i.e. not mixed) logistic 
models.

The most surprising findings to me:

 1. Estimates of the treatment effect seem to be biased towards zero in
    logistic models.
 2. But these estimates are closer to the true parameter value when
    controlling for a relevant covariate. (Which would increase power.)
 3. However, the treatment estimate has a larger standard error when
    adjusting for a covariate! (Which would decrease power!)
 4. On the whole, adjusting for a covariate enhances power, though - or
    at least doesn't substantially decrease power given a decent-size
    sample.

This may all be common knowledge to those of you with a stronger 
background in statistics, but it was new to me. You'll find the 
simulation code and a full write-up at my blog - let me know if I've 
made any blunders :) - 
http://janhove.github.io/analysis/2015/07/17/covariate-adjustment-logistic-regression/

Cheers,
Jan

