I like knitr. Most folks in my lab like mark-down, but I like to have all
the other Latex bells and whistles, which I'm sure you need for your book.
I find the conversion to be rather seamless. I find knitr with latex a lot
easier to configure (e.g., the global switching of our R prompts, output,
etc. are formatted in latex, the switching on/off of input/output display
etc.). Knitr is also faster (b/c of relatively intelligent caching [though
occasionally there are problems with that]). All my LSA stats slides were
done in knitr: http://wiki.bcs.rochester.edu/HlpLab/LSA2013Regression

Also have a look at this page that Dave Kleinschmidt put together with a
quick intro to Knitr: http://wiki.bcs.rochester.edu/HlpLab/LabmeetingSP13w13,
which contains a list of the best features and some problems, as well as
further templates and examples.

hth,

Florian

