Hi Roger and all,

[I can't figure out how to respond in-line, hence the fresh email].

IIRC, the Sweave caching setup was really annoying, because it kept
creating new cached files every time I changed something. This was many
years ago. So I just stopped using caching.  knitr makes it easy, because
you just set cache=TRUE or FALSE and that's it. You can also set up
dependencies so that if one chunk is updated, the dependent chunk is also
recomputed.

Apart from that, the big advantage of using knitr for me was that I could
reuse all my Sweave+LaTeX code (hundreds of pages of lecture notes)
virtually without any change. With Markdown there seems to be some
gymnastics required.

I don't even use Markdown for beamer presentations because there are no
page numbers by default, and one has to bend over backwards to get that
working. I just take a beamer .tex template and add knitr functionality.

It was Baayen and Bates who kind of forced me into knitr, for the
Parsimonious paper. Initially I was really unhappy because of the ```
syntax for R code, it looks so ugly. But then I eventually found out that I
could use <<>>= @ syntax in knitr as well. I write my papers now using apa6
and knitr, an example is:

https://github.com/vasishth/BayesLMMTutorial

For book length lecture notes, I really like the Tufte style and knitr,
because I can use the margin to show figures without interrupting the flow.
I will be checking in an example here:

https://github.com/vasishth/LM

These are my linear modeling notes (both the Sweave and knitr versions are
there right now, I will eliminate Sweave soon), which I use in a course
here at Potsdam for the MSc in Cognitive Systems.

best,

--
Shravan Vasishth
Professor for Psycholinguistics and Neurolinguistics
Department of Linguistics
University of Potsdam, Germany
http://www.ling.uni-potsdam.de/~vasishth

