From dan-uZj4hkX1dquEi8DpZVb4nw at public.gmane.org  Tue Jul 21 18:56:48 2015
From: dan-uZj4hkX1dquEi8DpZVb4nw at public.gmane.org (Dan Mirman)
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2015 14:56:48 -0400
Subject: parsimonious MLR
Message-ID: <CAGfVdyqHeDVQr53ibRR91LfxOAijmv7og+=FCxvLTPmq+zx+5w@mail.gmail.com>

Hello list,
I tried the new RePsychLing package for building parsimonious mixed models (
http://arxiv.org/abs/1506.04967) on my data and reached a somewhat
confusing situation. I've put the details on RPubs (
http://rpubs.com/danmirman/repsychling_test1); the short version is that
rePCA() is telling me that I should simplify the random effects, but model
comparison (LRT) is telling me that the goodness of fit is reduced when I
do that. In one of the cases I tried, I was removing random effect
correlations of 1.0, which certainly seem like good candidates for removal,
but nevertheless reduced goodness of fit.

Has anyone else encountered this kind of situation? I like the general idea
of "parsimonious" random effect structures and my intuition was that
rePCA() would be a more conservative approach than LRT (i.e., would produce
more inclusive random effect structures), but it looks like the opposite is
happening. Any advice about how to resolve the disagreement between rePCA()
and LRT?

(In case anyone cares, the example data sets were just two that I had near
to hand and were not selected based on being particularly clean or having
any tricky issues).

Thanks,
Dan

-- 
