From kliegl-KiJTPfjSHVUubQSw3dWLiw at public.gmane.org  Tue Jul 21 19:26:07 2015
From: kliegl-KiJTPfjSHVUubQSw3dWLiw at public.gmane.org (Reinhold Kliegl)
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2015 21:26:07 +0200
Subject: Re: parsimonious MLR
In-Reply-To: <CAGfVdyqHeDVQr53ibRR91LfxOAijmv7og+=FCxvLTPmq+zx+5w@mail.gmail.com>
References: <CAGfVdyqHeDVQr53ibRR91LfxOAijmv7og+=FCxvLTPmq+zx+5w@mail.gmail.com>
Message-ID: <7801DDC1-1C5D-4454-A2A4-64560862A508@uni-potsdam.de>

Yes, I have seen this occasionally, too. The short answer is do not trust LRT statistics involving an overparameterized (degenerate) model.  So the first step is to make sure that your model parameters are supported by the data; the rePCA() function is very useful to this end. The LRT-based degree of parsimony within a chain of identified models may depend also on where on the exploratory-confirmatory dimension you locate your analysis with respect to the variance components and correlation parameters of your model.

