Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2015 13:23:31 +0200
Subject: Announcing paper on Determiner Omission analyzed with GLMMs
Message-ID: <2ED9A7C3-237A-4E97-B2EF-CA189F9EA7A7@linguistics.rub.de>

Dear Members of the R-Lang list, 

hopefully, it is not inappropriate to use this channel for self-advertisement, but I would like to point to my paper on Determiner Omission in German PPs, which is available through 

http://www.linguistics.ruhr-uni-bochum.de/~kiss/publications/Determiner_Omission.pdf

It is an application of GLMMs (random intercept models) to the question whether a determiner can be omitted inside a PP (famous, yet improper cases from English are "by car" or "in school"). The paper is rather lengthy (67 pp.) but this is due to the explanation and illustration of the senses of the prepositions involved. 

Here is the abstract:

In this paper, we present a novel approach to so-called determinerless PPs, i.e. prepositional phrases that allow singular count nouns to occur without an accompanying determiner, despite other rules in the grammar requiring the presence of the determiner. The construction is confined to complements of prepositions in many languages, and has received some attention in the past years. We will start by distinguishing a broader and a smaller class of determinerless PPs and analyse the smaller class on the basis of annotated corpora and methods from statistical classification, generalized linear mixed modelling in particular. The analysis is based on data sets for two couples of German prepositions, viz. mit (‘with’) and ohne (‘without’), and über (‘over, above’) and unter (‘under, below’).

Perhaps the paper is of interest to one or the other member of the list.

With kind regards

Tibor



   	 	
